f plausible outcomes.", "watch": "The first credible general-election polls, the eventual nominees, and spending in the concurrent governor's race could move the margin in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Georgia enters the 2028 presidential race as one of the closest states in the country. Recent cycles have been decided by a point or two, and the state's partisan fundamentals give Republicans only a slight edge. The contest is open, since the outgoing president is term-limited, so neither party carries an incumbency advantage.\n\nRace-specific evidence remains scarce: no public general-election polls, expert ratings, or market pricing are available. The Democratic side is still unsettled, with Jon Ossoff deflecting presidential speculation during his Senate race while attention focuses on the governor's contest. With little beyond fundamentals to go on, the forecast treats Georgia as a genuine tossup leaning narrowly Republican, where turnout, the nominees, or campaign spending could easily decide the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4567, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4683, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.467, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4627, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4625, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4617, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4629, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4605, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.4643, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.4656, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.4556, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.455, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "